<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasily Dolgorukov-Krymsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1722">1722</a>–<a href="/years/1782">1782</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Vasily Mikhailovich Dolgorukov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasily Mikhailovich Dolgorukov was a Russian general who commanded the Second Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1768–1774. In 1771 he stormed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perekop">Perekop</a> lines and occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimean peninsula</a>, taking Kefe and other Ottoman fortresses, for which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> gave him the honorific surname Krymsky ('of Crimea'). He later served as governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasily-dolgorukov-krymsky.jpg" alt="Vasily Dolgorukov-Krymsky" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Roslin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dolgorukov-Krymsky_Vasily_Michailovich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
